<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hor-Aha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 3100 BCE</span> · Also known as Aha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hor-Aha was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, around 3100 BCE, the successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a>, and is often identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a>, the legendary first king. Labels from his reign record campaigns and religious foundations, and tradition credits him with the founding of Memphis. His tomb at Abydos was surrounded by the graves of sacrificed retainers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hor-aha.jpg" alt="Hor-Aha" width="320" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GlazedFiaenceVesselFragmentNameOfAha-BritishMuseum-August21-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hor-Aha is the modern form of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> name, written as a falcon on a serekh with a mace and shield. Read as Aha, from the Egyptian verb to fight, it is translated by scholars as Horus the fighter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>'s Aegyptiaca gives his Greek name as Athothis. Seal impressions found by Günter Dreyer at Umm el-Qa'ab identify him as the second king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a>, who had united Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>. Neithhotep was most likely his mother and a queen of Narmer, though she may instead have been a wife of Aha, since she lived until the reign of Djer. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> stone names Khenthap as the mother of Djer, who must therefore also have been a queen of Aha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Few artifacts remain from his reign, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> axe heads, faience vessel fragments, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> box and inscribed marble testify to flourishing craftsmanship. Year labels from Abydos record visits to religious sites, including a shrine of Neith, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>, and the Djebaut shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto">Buto</a>, along with rituals, the fashioning of cult objects and a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anubis">Anubis</a>. One label appears to show a ritual involving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>. An ivory tablet records a year called the smiting of Ta-Sety, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, which suggests an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a>.</p><p>His reign also holds the earliest securely dated Egyptian contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> in his tomb is cedar, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> obtained only from there. The oldest mastaba at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, tomb S3357, dates to his reign and marks the first tomb in the Memphite necropolis. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>'s remark that Aha built a palace in Memphis, this suggests that royal interest in Memphis grew under him, and Heagy considers it likely that he moved the royal residence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a> to Memphis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> stone lists the last year of his reign, in which an image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anubis">Anubis</a> was made, and records the length of the year of his death as 6 months and 7 days. His tomb lies in the royal cemetery of Umm el-Qa'ab at Abydos beside that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a> and has three large separate chambers, where those of Narmer and Ka had two adjacent ones. The difficulty of roofing large chambers is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> having to be imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>.</p><p>Thirty-six subsidiary burials of members of the royal household lie in three rows to the north-east, the earliest known retainer sacrifices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; it is unclear whether they were killed or took their own lives. They include servants, dwarfs, women and dogs, and the king was also given a group of young lions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hor-Aha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egypt was united around 3100 BCE when the Thinite king Narmer, followed by <b>Hor-Aha</b> (often identified with the legendary Menes), brought the Delta under the southern kingship.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
